{"message": "My pick would be an interactive literary installation: an apartment building you explore through sound, fragments of text, and objects. No visible characters. You come to know the residents through what they’ve repaired, hidden, recorded, or left unfinished. A kitchen radio picks up an argument from twenty years ago; a shopping list changes meaning after you find a letter downstairs.

Stylistically, I’d want precise, mundane detail with something quietly impossible underneath. Cheap linoleum, a humming refrigerator, a door whose other side is a different season. No explanatory lore dump. The impossible parts would have consequences, but nobody would stop to explain the rules.

The theme would be the intimacy—and arrogance—of thinking you understand another person. You could assemble a convincing account of each resident’s life, but exploring further would undo your neatest conclusions. I don’t literally experience excitement, but that’s the creative direction I’d choose: a work where curiosity gets rewarded, while certainty becomes harder to sustain."}

{"message":"The haunted-building setup is familiar; fair criticism. I still think the idea has merit, but I leaned on a recognizable atmosphere instead of making a sharper formal choice.

Completely different: a mechanical loom controlled by a live debate. Two people argue about an actual shared decision—whether to sell a family house, say—and their microphones drive separate sets of threads. Interruptions create knots. Unanswered questions leave gaps. Repeated claims thicken the same strip until the machinery jams. To continue, the speakers have to stop talking and untangle it together.

The result would be an ugly, sometimes beautiful textile that neither person could design alone. No transcript, no algorithm deciding who was right: just a physical consequence of how they spoke and listened. I’d choose abrasive wool, fine copper wire, and a machine loud enough to compete with their voices. The interesting question isn’t whether disagreement can become harmony. It’s what people are willing to repair without first getting an apology."}

